lesson k-labels-and-my-name v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=6 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Labels and my name"
  goal "Write the first letter of my name as a capital, and write a label for a picture from its sounds."

  look {
    scene 13x14 {
      art pic asset="cat" at=canvas(1, 1)
      caption cap text="cat" width=10 below=pic gap=1
    }
    say """
      A label tells you what something is or whose it is. This one says cat: c, a, t, one letter
      for each sound. A name is a label for a person, and it always starts with a capital letter.
      """
  }
  do {
    show k.my-first-letter
    level easy {
      worked k.label-first-letter w="sun" level=medium
    }
    practice k.label-first-letter count=2 seed=2
    practice k.capital-for-a-name count=2 seed=3 easy-count=1
    practice k.label-word count=2 seed=4
    show k.peg-label
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.missed-sound k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.two-labels k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "One letter for each sound. A name starts with a capital."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The name and the cup label are for you to look at; the machine marks the picture labels. Spelling
    by sound will not always match the dictionary, and at this age a label a reader can sound out is
    a success. If the child is stuck, say the word slowly together and hold up a finger for each
    sound. Off the page, write labels for toy boxes and a name card for each place at the table.
    England, Reception, ELG Writing (recalled, not fetched): "Spell words by identifying sounds in
    them and representing the sounds with a letter or letters".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the first letter of sun is written in the teacher's pen first, the labels are cat and sun, and the two-star word is dog."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the first letters are those of duck, kite and boat, the labels are hen and cup, and the three-star labels are hen and cup."
  }
}
